# Jobs exempt from the "every Linux job is profiled" invariant enforced by
# .ci/scripts/quality/check-profiler-coverage.sh.
#
# Entry format: <workflow-basename>.yml:<job-id>, one per line, each covered by
# a "# BLOCKER: <reason>" comment block above it (a blank line resets the
# tracked BLOCKER, so one reason covers the contiguous group beneath it; plain
# `#` comment lines do NOT reset it, which is how the per-workflow subheadings
# below stay inside their group). Reasons are parsed and quality-checked by the
# shared validator in .ci/scripts/lib/blocker-validator.sh, exactly like every
# other allowlist in this repo -- see docs/agent/suppressions.md.
#
# THIS LIST CAN ONLY SHRINK. The gate re-derives its own oracle every run and
# rejects an entry the moment it stops suppressing something real: a job that
# no longer exists, a job that is now profiled, a reusable-workflow caller, or
# a job that does not run on Linux. That is the liveness half of the BLOCKER
# convention, enforced in the gate itself rather than by an external probe,
# because the oracle IS the parse the gate already performs.

# BLOCKER: breakpoint.yml is in the frozen set hashed by .ci/breakpoint/MANIFEST.sha256 (workflow/breakpoint.yml) and must stay byte-identical in every repository that vendors breakpoint; check-breakpoint-drift.sh cmp's the live copy against the template, so a console-only profiler step would fail that gate. The session job is also runner-agnostic by design (runs-on is the dispatch input, chosen per session), so there is no single runner-label to arm the HOST_LEAK check with.
breakpoint.yml:preflight
breakpoint.yml:session

# BLOCKER: these are the profiler probe's own experiment arms and adding a direct profiler step would destroy the measurement. facts-slim/facts-latest run the sampler in --probe form only, on purpose: they answer what a job inside the ubuntu-slim container can read from /sys/fs/cgroup, and a second sampler in the same job would contend with the one being measured. post-nested-slim/post-nested-latest are the TREATMENT arm of the nesting question (does a JavaScript action's post: hook fire when nested inside a composite?); giving them a direct uses: as well would make a panel appear whether or not nesting works, which is precisely the thing the probe exists to distinguish.
profiler-probe.yml:facts-latest
profiler-probe.yml:facts-slim
profiler-probe.yml:post-nested-latest
profiler-probe.yml:post-nested-slim

# BLOCKER: rollout ledger for the runner profiler, which landed 2026-08-05 with one live job (profiler-probe.yml:post-direct-latest) and 90 jobs still unwired. Enumerated rather than waived so the gate is BLOCKING for every job added from today on, and so the burn-down is a visible diff instead of a habit: each line is deleted by the commit that adds the profiler step to that job, and the gate rejects the line the moment the job is covered, so a forgotten deletion is a build failure rather than dead weight. The shape of the burn-down depends on the answer profiler-probe.yml is dispatched to get -- if a nested post: hook fires, one uses: in setup-workspace covers most of these at once and COVERING_ACTIONS in the gate grows by one line; if it does not, each job needs its own edit.
